================================================================================
GLOBAL ITEM MASTER — CUSTOMER / PART / MODEL NUMBER AND CUSTOM SPECIFICATIONS
================================================================================

This section is a mandatory addition to the complete specification. Preserve all existing requirements except where this section explicitly changes a label, page placement, or behavior.

1. RENAME THE ITEM MASTER PAGE
- The page/menu label “Global Item Master & Import / Export” must be changed to “Global Item Master”.
- Use “Global Item Master” consistently in navigation, page titles, breadcrumbs, dashboard links, permissions labels, and related user-facing references.
- Import and Export functionality must remain available within the Global Item Master experience. Renaming the page must not remove or disable import/export.
- Do not retain “Global Item Master & Import / Export” as a visible page title or menu label.

2. RENAME THE CREATE ITEM ACTION
- Change “Create Item (Maker)” to “Create Item”.
- Apply the new label consistently to navigation, buttons, page headings, breadcrumbs, links, and relevant help text.
- Preserve the existing Create Item functionality and permissions.

3. RENAME “PART / MODEL NUMBER”
- Change the field/attribute label “Part / Model Number” to “Customer / Part / Model Number”.
- Apply this label consistently wherever this field appears, including item creation, item details, item search, configuration, import/export mappings, validation messages, and generated descriptions where the label is shown.
- Preserve the existing stored value and data mapping where possible. Use a controlled migration or backward-compatible alias if the current field is already persisted; do not discard existing Part / Model Number values.
- Support customer-specific part/model references without assuming that the same customer part number is globally unique. Use the appropriate customer identity and uniqueness rules defined by the application.

4. MOVE CUSTOMER / MANUFACTURER / BRAND (MASTER) AND CUSTOMER / PART / MODEL NUMBER TO A SEPARATE CUSTOM SPECIFICATIONS PAGE
- Move the “Customer / Manufacturer / Brand (Master)” configuration/maintenance function and the “Customer / Part / Model Number” configuration/maintenance function into a dedicated page named “Custom Specifications”.
- “Custom Specifications” must be a separate page within the Global Item Master area/module. It must not be mixed into the main Global Item Master list as inline master-maintenance sections.
- The Global Item Master navigation should provide a clear, permission-controlled route to “Custom Specifications”.
- The Custom Specifications page must contain distinct, clearly labelled sections or tabs for:
  A. Customer / Manufacturer / Brand (Master)
  B. Customer / Part / Model Number
- Keep the two specification types logically separate, with their own forms, lists, validation, search, sorting, filtering, status, and create/edit/deactivate actions as appropriate.
- Provide relationships between customer, manufacturer/brand, and customer-specific part/model references only where required by the configured business rules. Do not automatically treat Customer, Manufacturer, and Brand as interchangeable entities.
- Support active/inactive or equivalent lifecycle status. Do not hard-delete master records that are referenced by existing item records; use controlled deactivation and preserve referential integrity.
- Maintain audit history for create, edit, deactivate, and reactivation operations, including the actor, timestamp, and changed fields, according to the application's existing audit policy.
- Respect role-based access control. Users who can view Global Item Master must not automatically receive permission to modify Custom Specifications unless granted the appropriate permission.

5. CUSTOMER / MANUFACTURER / BRAND MASTER REQUIREMENTS
- Provide a searchable and pageable master list with sorting and filters appropriate to the available fields.
- Support create, view, edit, activate/deactivate, duplicate detection, and validation based on authorized business rules.
- Where applicable, capture entity type explicitly (Customer, Manufacturer, Brand) rather than relying on ambiguous names.
- Validate required fields, normalized names, duplicate identities, and references before saving.
- Do not invent mandatory fields that are not established by the configured business rules; allow administrators to configure required fields where the existing configuration framework supports it.

6. CUSTOMER / PART / MODEL NUMBER REQUIREMENTS
- Provide a searchable, sortable, filterable, pageable list of customer-specific part/model references.
- Support the relevant relationships to Customer, Manufacturer, Brand, and Global Item Master records when configured and applicable.
- Allow a single global item to have multiple customer-specific part/model references where business rules permit.
- Validate uniqueness using the configured business key (for example, customer + part/model number, and manufacturer/brand if explicitly included in the business key); do not impose global uniqueness on the part/model number alone unless explicitly configured.
- Preserve the customer-provided reference separately from the internally generated Global Item Code.
- Do not silently replace the Global Item Code with a customer part/model number or alter an already-issued Global Item Code when these references are changed.
- Make the relationship and matching behavior visible to authorized users so that customer part/model references can be used in item lookup and import matching without creating accidental duplicate global items.

7. GLOBAL ITEM MASTER INTEGRATION
- The Global Item Master create/edit/detail experience must show the applicable Customer / Part / Model Number references and manufacturer/brand references as related data, when the user has permission to view them.
- Do not duplicate the master-maintenance forms inside the Global Item Master item form. Provide links or selectors that open or reference the dedicated Custom Specifications page/function.
- Where users need to select an existing customer, manufacturer, brand, or customer part/model reference while creating or importing an item, use searchable selectors backed by authorized master records.
- Define behavior for missing or inactive references: display a clear validation error and offer the permitted path to request or create the required reference, without silently inventing a master record.
- Ensure import/export templates and column-mapping configuration can map customer/manufacturer/brand and customer part/model references. Include validation, duplicate handling, and an error report for invalid or unresolved references.
- Ensure item search can find an item by its Global Item Code and, when authorized and configured, by Customer / Part / Model Number, customer, manufacturer, or brand.
- Enforce access control and tenant/branch/company scope for all lookups, imports, exports, and API endpoints.

8. GLOBAL ITEM MASTER SEARCH, SORT, FILTER, PAGINATION, AND BULK IMPORT
- Retain the existing requirements to rename “Global Item Master & Import / Export” to “Global Item Master” and “Create Item (Maker)” to “Create Item”.
- Retain the requirement to make Bulk Import actually work end to end. Diagnose the real defect in the existing upload, parsing, mapping, validation, preview, persistence, job processing, and reporting path; do not merely redesign the upload button or guess at the root cause.
- Provide search, sort, and filter support for Global Item Code, Category, Generated Description, Branch Availability, Governance Status, SAP Status, and Action-related capabilities where appropriate. “Action” is an operation column and must not be treated as an item attribute filter unless a concrete, meaningful action filter is defined.
- Provide multiple-page support using reliable server-side pagination, stable sorting, accurate totals, page-size selection, and retained search/filter state when navigating pages.
- Keep all existing security, validation, audit, code-generation, description-generation, item-governance, and supported SAP integration requirements.

9. NAVIGATION AND LABEL CONSISTENCY
- The relevant navigation hierarchy should clearly expose:
  - Global Item Master
  - Create Item
  - Custom Specifications
  - Import / Export functions within Global Item Master
- Do not create duplicate page labels for the same function.
- Update route titles, breadcrumbs, dashboard shortcuts, permission/resource labels, contextual links, and help text to match the final names.
- Preserve backward-compatible routes or redirects where needed, without continuing to show the old labels to users.
- Do not introduce Stage 0 Review, Page Acceptance Workflow, page-by-page acceptance gates, or any requirement for image.png. The previously defined global overrides remain in force.

10. ACCEPTANCE TESTS
- The visible page title and navigation label are “Global Item Master”.
- The visible Create Item label is “Create Item”.
- The field/attribute label is “Customer / Part / Model Number”.
- “Customer / Manufacturer / Brand (Master)” and “Customer / Part / Model Number” maintenance are accessible from the separate “Custom Specifications” page.
- The Global Item Master list remains focused on global items; custom specification master maintenance is not embedded as a duplicated inline maintenance page.
- Existing customer part/model values remain available after the label and page changes.
- An item can be searched using its customer part/model reference when permitted and configured.
- Bulk import is tested from upload through durable persistence and row-level outcome reporting, with evidence of successful and failed test cases.
- Search, sorting, filters, and multiple-page navigation work together and preserve the selected criteria.
- SAP integration uses supported interfaces and does not write directly to SAP database tables.
- No Stage 0 review, page acceptance workflow, or image.png dependency is reintroduced.
- Do not state that these requirements have been implemented or tested unless the actual application has been changed and the relevant tests have passed.

END OF GLOBAL ITEM MASTER / CUSTOM SPECIFICATIONS ADDITION
